WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.974 --> 00:00:09.253
LILY (ECHOES): Aldie.

2
00:00:09.276 --> 00:00:10.753
PARKER: Careful. Lily.

3
00:00:10.778 --> 00:00:12.422
Parker. you have to talk to somebody.

4
00:00:12.445 --> 00:00:13.957
Especially if you're seeing ghosts.

5
00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:15.259
Her name is Lily.

6
00:00:15.282 --> 00:00:16.425
Who is she?

7
00:00:16.449 --> 00:00:19.096
That's the problem. I have no idea.

8
00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:21.780
I'm ready. I guess. to... to talk

9
00:00:21.803 --> 00:00:24.468
<i>about how your mom really died.</i>

10
00:00:24.492 --> 00:00:27.626
<i>She drove off in a rage
with me hollering</i>

11
00:00:27.649 --> 00:00:28.992
<i>to never come back.</i>

12
00:00:29.016 --> 00:00:31.940
<i>PARKER:
Turns out. Lily is not a ghost.</i>

13
00:00:32.871 --> 00:00:34.374
She's real.

14
00:00:35.308 --> 00:00:37.947
So. I found his mother's
original death certificate.

15
00:00:38.436 --> 00:00:39.715
Jess.

16
00:00:39.738 --> 00:00:41.774
something doesn't add up.

17
00:00:46.131 --> 00:00:48.042
(KNOCKING ON DOOR)

18
00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.993
MAN: Please. I just want to talk.

19
00:00:51.017 --> 00:00:52.762
- (POUNDING ON DOOR)
- I knew you were in there.

20
00:00:52.786 --> 00:00:55.530
Open this door or I'll open it for you!

21
00:00:55.554 --> 00:00:56.566
(WOMAN WHIMPERS)

22
00:00:56.590 --> 00:00:57.899
You think I wouldn't find you?

23
00:00:57.923 --> 00:00:59.468
You're too late. She's long gone.

24
00:00:59.491 --> 00:01:02.804
- Where is she?
- Somewhere you'll never find her.

25
00:01:02.829 --> 00:01:04.974
- Yeah. we'll see about that.
- (SCREAMS)

26
00:01:04.998 --> 00:01:07.533
(BOTH STRUGGLING)

27
00:01:13.506 --> 00:01:15.483
MAN: Where is she?

28
00:01:15.507 --> 00:01:17.186
I'll teach you to stick
your nose in my business.

29
00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:19.054
WOMAN: You don't scare me.

30
00:01:19.078 --> 00:01:20.956
MAN: Where the hell is she?!

31
00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:23.149
(WOMAN GASPING)

32
00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.588
♪ ♪

33
00:01:48.972 --> 00:01:56.972
Synced & corrected by -robtor-
www.addic7ed.com

34
00:01:59.252 --> 00:02:01.162
Good morning. Santa's helpers.

35
00:02:01.186 --> 00:02:02.998
Uh. yeah. that's us.

36
00:02:03.022 --> 00:02:05.167
- TORRES: Ho ho ho.
- Oh. come on.

37
00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:07.069
Nick. this toy drive is your baby.

38
00:02:07.093 --> 00:02:08.824
So let's get in the spirit because...

39
00:02:08.848 --> 00:02:12.074
I just scored the hottest. hard-to-find.

40
00:02:12.098 --> 00:02:15.243
must-have gift of the season
for one lucky kid.

41
00:02:15.268 --> 00:02:16.711
Give a guess what it is.

42
00:02:16.735 --> 00:02:18.713
BOTH: Blaze the Dragon.

43
00:02:18.737 --> 00:02:20.549
Triangle box gave it away?

44
00:02:20.573 --> 00:02:24.019
That. and apparently
the must-have gift of the year

45
00:02:24.043 --> 00:02:25.475
is not that hard to find after all.

46
00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:27.415
We have three Blazes?

47
00:02:27.439 --> 00:02:29.151
Make that four.

48
00:02:29.175 --> 00:02:31.554
And I was so proud
that I found this. too.

49
00:02:31.578 --> 00:02:33.555
Oh. more the merrier. I guess.

50
00:02:33.579 --> 00:02:36.457
I am also here to see if your
ears are burning. Agent Knight.

51
00:02:36.481 --> 00:02:38.227
Or. or is that tingling?

52
00:02:38.251 --> 00:02:39.461
Is someone talking about me?

53
00:02:39.485 --> 00:02:41.362
Your CO at NCIS: ELITE

54
00:02:41.387 --> 00:02:43.432
just called to update me
on your training.

55
00:02:43.456 --> 00:02:46.801
which she claims that you were...
how did she put it?

56
00:02:46.825 --> 00:02:48.103
"crushing"?

57
00:02:48.127 --> 00:02:49.538
Really?

58
00:02:49.562 --> 00:02:51.073
You sound surprised.

59
00:02:51.097 --> 00:02:53.408
No. No. I'm not... I just...

60
00:02:53.432 --> 00:02:55.677
Thank you. I love training.

61
00:02:55.701 --> 00:02:57.947
I'm just really kind of starting
to chomp at the bit

62
00:02:57.971 --> 00:02:59.549
- for that first assignment.
- Well.

63
00:02:59.573 --> 00:03:01.415
just be patient.
because like these Blazes.

64
00:03:01.439 --> 00:03:04.286
you'll wish for one.
you'll end up with four.

65
00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:08.090
Well. actually. Director. I believe
that Blaze makes. uh. number five.

66
00:03:08.114 --> 00:03:10.491
Yeah. great minds think alike.

67
00:03:10.515 --> 00:03:12.294
Well. maybe next year our great minds

68
00:03:12.318 --> 00:03:13.763
can make a plan and coordinate.

69
00:03:13.787 --> 00:03:15.497
So. where is Agent Parker anyway?

70
00:03:15.521 --> 00:03:17.132
Autopsy. With Jimmy.

71
00:03:17.156 --> 00:03:19.467
Kind of why we're not
in such a jolly mood.

72
00:03:19.491 --> 00:03:21.704
That's right. Parker's mother.

73
00:03:21.728 --> 00:03:22.872
They exhumed her remains.

74
00:03:22.896 --> 00:03:25.908
MCGEE: And Jimmy's getting
back the autopsy results now.

75
00:03:25.931 --> 00:03:27.877
Talk about a Christmas buzzkill.

76
00:03:27.901 --> 00:03:31.713
Well. let's just all hope that Parker
gets the closure that he needs.

77
00:03:31.737 --> 00:03:33.337
Amen to that.

78
00:03:34.873 --> 00:03:36.789
I assumed you didn't want
to see the actual...

79
00:03:36.813 --> 00:03:39.153
No. No. Jimmy. I don't.

80
00:03:39.536 --> 00:03:42.991
Good. because. 50 years. huh?

81
00:03:43.015 --> 00:03:45.627
Is she wearing any rings
or anything resembling

82
00:03:45.651 --> 00:03:47.597
a-an heirloom?

83
00:03:47.621 --> 00:03:48.831
She's wearing a dress.

84
00:03:48.854 --> 00:03:50.164
Kind of blue. Why?

85
00:03:50.188 --> 00:03:52.501
I don't know.
I'm just looking for some...

86
00:03:52.525 --> 00:03:53.902
connection.

87
00:03:53.926 --> 00:03:56.052
Um... Yeah.

88
00:03:56.596 --> 00:03:58.106
I just. uh...

89
00:03:58.131 --> 00:04:00.141
spent most of my life thinking of her

90
00:04:00.165 --> 00:04:02.510
running out on me and my sister.
and then here we are.

91
00:04:02.534 --> 00:04:04.813
all of a sudden
in the same room. It's...

92
00:04:04.837 --> 00:04:07.817
You know. this may be your
mother's body. Agent Parker.

93
00:04:07.841 --> 00:04:09.985
but it's not her. this is not...

94
00:04:10.009 --> 00:04:11.552
her spirit.

95
00:04:11.576 --> 00:04:13.155
Her spirit lives on in your memory.

96
00:04:13.179 --> 00:04:16.557
I-I. I know. this sounds like
standard grief-speak.

97
00:04:16.581 --> 00:04:18.728
but I believe it.

98
00:04:18.752 --> 00:04:21.029
I appreciate that. Jimmy. I do.

99
00:04:21.053 --> 00:04:22.865
It's just that. uh...

100
00:04:22.889 --> 00:04:25.701
the few memories I have of my mother

101
00:04:25.725 --> 00:04:27.500
aren't exactly...

102
00:04:27.766 --> 00:04:30.011
Norman Rockwell material.

103
00:04:30.036 --> 00:04:31.045
Hmm.

104
00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:32.875
Onto the findings.

105
00:04:33.215 --> 00:04:35.209
Yeah. sure. What do we got?

106
00:04:35.233 --> 00:04:36.678
JIMMY: We've got a broken clavicle.

107
00:04:36.702 --> 00:04:39.447
bruised sternum. severe burns.

108
00:04:39.471 --> 00:04:41.516
Most everything consistent
with a car crash

109
00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:44.353
- and the resulting fire.
- Most everything?

110
00:04:44.377 --> 00:04:46.822
Yes. the anomaly being
these two crush injuries

111
00:04:46.846 --> 00:04:48.356
to the occipital bone.

112
00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:52.226
Caused by a cylindrical object
such as bat or a pipe.

113
00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:54.396
The original coroner's report...

114
00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:55.764
Was accurate.

115
00:04:55.788 --> 00:04:57.199
Yeah. the depth of these injuries

116
00:04:57.223 --> 00:05:00.059
would've been immediately fatal.

117
00:05:00.826 --> 00:05:03.161
Your mother was most likely murdered.

118
00:05:04.096 --> 00:05:05.240
Murdered.

119
00:05:05.264 --> 00:05:06.742
(DOOR OPENS)

120
00:05:06.766 --> 00:05:08.810
Kase? You okay?

121
00:05:08.834 --> 00:05:11.045
Yeah. I just didn't want to interrupt.

122
00:05:11.069 --> 00:05:13.548
No. no. please. Interrupt.

123
00:05:13.572 --> 00:05:15.016
(KASIE CLEARS THROAT)

124
00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:16.819
PARKER: Unless. uh.

125
00:05:16.843 --> 00:05:18.286
you've got worse news.

126
00:05:18.310 --> 00:05:19.788
Worse? No. i-it...

127
00:05:19.812 --> 00:05:21.456
It honestly might not even be that bad.

128
00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:22.757
- depending on...
- PARKER: Just...

129
00:05:22.781 --> 00:05:24.149
just say it.

130
00:05:24.790 --> 00:05:26.457
That's not your mother.

131
00:05:28.754 --> 00:05:30.932
- Say that again?
- Her tissue sample

132
00:05:30.956 --> 00:05:33.334
came back inconclusive for alcohol.

133
00:05:33.358 --> 00:05:34.803
And since drunk driving

134
00:05:34.827 --> 00:05:36.771
was listed as the cause for the crash.

135
00:05:36.795 --> 00:05:37.939
I ran her DNA

136
00:05:37.963 --> 00:05:39.875
and it wasn't a match to you

137
00:05:39.899 --> 00:05:42.576
or to the sample
provided by your sister.

138
00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:44.045
Okay. so...

139
00:05:44.069 --> 00:05:45.781
the cemetery sent over
the wrong remains.

140
00:05:45.805 --> 00:05:47.649
I mean. that does happen.

141
00:05:47.673 --> 00:05:49.317
KASIE: It does. but it didn't.

142
00:05:49.341 --> 00:05:50.952
I checked.

143
00:05:50.976 --> 00:05:52.387
With the cemetery and the local ME.

144
00:05:52.411 --> 00:05:53.822
Everybody did everything right.

145
00:05:53.846 --> 00:05:56.591
It's the right grave.
it's the right casket.

146
00:05:56.615 --> 00:05:58.427
it's just the wrong occupant.

147
00:05:58.451 --> 00:06:00.028
(SIGHS) So.

148
00:06:00.052 --> 00:06:02.262
this is not my mother.

149
00:06:03.601 --> 00:06:04.545
No.

150
00:06:04.581 --> 00:06:06.093
Who is this?

151
00:06:06.129 --> 00:06:08.197
I don't know. but I'm hoping that after

152
00:06:08.223 --> 00:06:10.769
- some further DNA testing...
- Further testing?

153
00:06:10.863 --> 00:06:13.809
If... I mean. if some maniac
crushed this woman's skull...

154
00:06:13.833 --> 00:06:17.103
whoever the hell she is...
what did they do with my mother?

155
00:06:17.903 --> 00:06:19.738
Where the hell is she?

156
00:06:27.146 --> 00:06:29.690
The delivery truck has
a few more stops to make.

157
00:06:29.714 --> 00:06:31.459
- so I won't be long.
- What do you mean?

158
00:06:31.483 --> 00:06:33.595
- I'm coming with you.
- No. no. you're not.

159
00:06:33.619 --> 00:06:37.156
Not when someone else
needs your help more than I do.

160
00:06:37.923 --> 00:06:39.990
KNIGHT: He's been staring
at his mother's accident report

161
00:06:40.014 --> 00:06:41.435
all morning.

162
00:06:41.459 --> 00:06:43.271
I hardly know what to say anymore.

163
00:06:43.295 --> 00:06:45.264
Well. you'll think of something.

164
00:06:55.007 --> 00:06:56.173
(CLEARS THROAT)

165
00:06:56.548 --> 00:06:58.209
Hey. Parker.

166
00:06:58.877 --> 00:07:00.288
You doing okay?

167
00:07:00.987 --> 00:07:04.125
No. Jess. I'm a pretty long way
from okay.

168
00:07:04.281 --> 00:07:05.860
Have you told Harriet?

169
00:07:05.884 --> 00:07:08.764
My sister's on a carrier in Guam.

170
00:07:09.329 --> 00:07:10.956
But thanks.

171
00:07:16.728 --> 00:07:18.908
So. unfortunately. the funeral home

172
00:07:18.932 --> 00:07:22.310
that handled your mother's
burial closed in 1983.

173
00:07:22.334 --> 00:07:24.112
I was able. though. to dig up

174
00:07:24.137 --> 00:07:26.314
the old accident report from the crash.

175
00:07:26.338 --> 00:07:28.350
Signed by Illinois State Trooper...

176
00:07:28.374 --> 00:07:31.509
first initial: L. last name: Burm.

177
00:07:32.911 --> 00:07:34.456
PARKER: Uh.
there's a trooper in the photo.

178
00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:35.858
but even if it's him.

179
00:07:35.882 --> 00:07:37.725
what are the chances
this guy's still alive?

180
00:07:37.750 --> 00:07:38.994
Pretty good. actually.

181
00:07:39.018 --> 00:07:40.661
Illinois State Police database has

182
00:07:40.685 --> 00:07:43.365
a "Sergeant Lester Burm"
listed as retired in '06.

183
00:07:43.389 --> 00:07:45.334
and nothing here about him being dead.

184
00:07:45.358 --> 00:07:48.737
Well. it might be a long shot.
but... nothing else to go on.

185
00:07:48.761 --> 00:07:51.340
Maybe the old trooper could
at least fill in some blanks?

186
00:07:51.363 --> 00:07:53.665
(PHONE RINGING)

187
00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:56.401
Yeah. Kase.

188
00:07:57.069 --> 00:07:58.747
Okay. I'm on my way.

189
00:07:58.872 --> 00:08:00.014
(SIGHS)

190
00:08:00.038 --> 00:08:01.716
Long shot. Sure.

191
00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:03.141
Why the hell not?

192
00:08:08.882 --> 00:08:10.625
What's up? You said it was urgent.

193
00:08:10.649 --> 00:08:13.728
Uh. no. I didn't say it was
urgent. I said it was exciting.

194
00:08:13.752 --> 00:08:16.064
Which sounds urgent. so what's up?

195
00:08:16.088 --> 00:08:17.432
What's up is we are building a face

196
00:08:17.456 --> 00:08:19.233
off the skull of our mystery woman.

197
00:08:19.257 --> 00:08:21.069
KASIE: 50 years later.
it still provides a blueprint.

198
00:08:21.093 --> 00:08:23.105
Tissue depth gives us
some general features...

199
00:08:23.129 --> 00:08:27.442
While I get to add details
like skin tone and expression.

200
00:08:27.466 --> 00:08:30.245
Wow. that is. uh... exciting.

201
00:08:30.269 --> 00:08:32.914
Uh. look. it... I mean.
it's a long shot. we know.

202
00:08:32.938 --> 00:08:35.918
Which now makes two
that I'm counting on.

203
00:08:35.942 --> 00:08:37.219
It's not that long a shot.

204
00:08:37.243 --> 00:08:39.520
I mean. this combined
with the DNA search

205
00:08:39.544 --> 00:08:41.056
from you and your sister

206
00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:42.624
could help us locate your mother.

207
00:08:42.648 --> 00:08:45.394
Plus. third long shot's the charm.

208
00:08:45.418 --> 00:08:46.961
Don't dismiss a long shot. Jimmy.

209
00:08:46.985 --> 00:08:49.163
You didn't find
the trooper already. did you?

210
00:08:49.187 --> 00:08:50.397
Not only did we find him.

211
00:08:50.421 --> 00:08:52.732
we found that he is currently
enjoying his retirement

212
00:08:52.758 --> 00:08:54.536
by driving a Winnebago
around the East Coast.

213
00:08:54.559 --> 00:08:56.171
And you're not gonna guess
how close he is.

214
00:08:56.195 --> 00:08:58.072
- The Navy Yard.
- Not that close.

215
00:08:58.096 --> 00:08:59.740
Sandy Point. the Maryland coast.

216
00:08:59.764 --> 00:09:02.010
And. uh. McGee is currently reaching out

217
00:09:02.034 --> 00:09:03.245
for an exact location.

218
00:09:03.269 --> 00:09:04.745
See? That is great.

219
00:09:04.769 --> 00:09:06.248
And who is this?

220
00:09:06.272 --> 00:09:09.018
The trooper who filed the
accident report on Parker's mom.

221
00:09:09.042 --> 00:09:11.485
I'm pinning my hopes on an octogenarian.

222
00:09:11.509 --> 00:09:13.422
Oh. no. his file says he's only 79.

223
00:09:13.446 --> 00:09:14.956
"Where there's life. there's hope."

224
00:09:14.980 --> 00:09:17.625
<i>- Lord of the Rings.</i>
- It worked on Samwise.

225
00:09:17.649 --> 00:09:19.260
Well. if it's good enough for Tolkien.

226
00:09:19.284 --> 00:09:21.254
it's better than nothing.

227
00:09:23.046 --> 00:09:24.765
- (SOFT CLATTERING)
<i>- BURM: I got to say. uh.</i>

228
00:09:24.789 --> 00:09:28.169
once I heard from your Agent McGee.

229
00:09:28.193 --> 00:09:30.304
I just would've gladly come to you.

230
00:09:30.328 --> 00:09:31.773
I mean. uh...

231
00:09:31.797 --> 00:09:34.308
retirement's okay. but I...

232
00:09:34.332 --> 00:09:36.534
sure do miss the action.

233
00:09:37.370 --> 00:09:39.780
That's a great-looking photo.
Looking snappy.

234
00:09:39.804 --> 00:09:41.615
I was. uh. making sergeant.

235
00:09:41.639 --> 00:09:43.652
A proud day.

236
00:09:43.676 --> 00:09:44.986
Pretty wife.

237
00:09:45.010 --> 00:09:47.556
Yeah. she sure was. Uh...

238
00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:49.658
Lost her a few years ago. but...

239
00:09:49.682 --> 00:09:52.126
Prettiest green eyes you've ever seen.

240
00:09:52.150 --> 00:09:53.628
Did Agent McGee mention why we were

241
00:09:53.652 --> 00:09:55.496
coming to see you. Mr. Burm?

242
00:09:55.520 --> 00:09:58.299
Call me Les. No. he didn't say. but...

243
00:09:58.323 --> 00:10:01.937
at my age. I assume
it's not about some new case.

244
00:10:01.961 --> 00:10:04.739
No. Les. this is one of your old cases.

245
00:10:04.763 --> 00:10:06.008
Oh.

246
00:10:06.032 --> 00:10:08.110
(LAUGHS): 1972. That's...

247
00:10:08.134 --> 00:10:10.678
That's not old. that's ancient.

248
00:10:10.702 --> 00:10:12.714
PARKER: It was a
fatal drunk-driving crash

249
00:10:12.738 --> 00:10:14.716
off Route 9 in Deerfield.

250
00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:16.418
God. I never saw this shot.

251
00:10:16.442 --> 00:10:19.053
Man. look at that
strapping young trooper.

252
00:10:19.077 --> 00:10:21.655
Still cocky and full of himself.

253
00:10:21.679 --> 00:10:23.357
(CHUCKLES)

254
00:10:23.381 --> 00:10:25.527
The truth is. I didn't know crap.

255
00:10:25.551 --> 00:10:28.697
The victim that was killed
in that crash was my mother.

256
00:10:28.721 --> 00:10:30.365
Eleanor Parker.

257
00:10:30.389 --> 00:10:31.732
Oh...

258
00:10:31.756 --> 00:10:33.534
G-Gosh. s-son. um.

259
00:10:33.558 --> 00:10:36.038
I didn't know this was personal.
I'm so sorry.

260
00:10:36.062 --> 00:10:39.397
No need to be sorry. just-just
try to remember. please.

261
00:10:39.873 --> 00:10:41.543
I mean. uh...

262
00:10:41.567 --> 00:10:45.047
I wrote up hundreds of crashes
like this over the years.

263
00:10:45.071 --> 00:10:47.349
but. I mean. this is so long ago...

264
00:10:47.373 --> 00:10:48.682
How about this little girl here?

265
00:10:48.706 --> 00:10:50.219
You don't remember seeing her?

266
00:10:50.243 --> 00:10:53.187
Mm. no. I don't remember anything.

267
00:10:53.211 --> 00:10:56.424
But look. that's my youthful
ineptitude right there.

268
00:10:56.448 --> 00:10:58.559
I mean. what the hell was I doing.

269
00:10:58.584 --> 00:11:00.095
letting a kid like that stand around

270
00:11:00.119 --> 00:11:02.230
with all the other lookie-loos?

271
00:11:02.254 --> 00:11:04.332
Any chance your ineptitude

272
00:11:04.356 --> 00:11:07.392
caused you to misidentify the victim?

273
00:11:08.460 --> 00:11:09.721
What?

274
00:11:09.746 --> 00:11:11.339
Parker.

275
00:11:12.730 --> 00:11:13.908
Uh. w-what are you saying?

276
00:11:13.932 --> 00:11:16.043
I mean. I'd never
make a mistake like that.

277
00:11:16.067 --> 00:11:18.212
Well. someone did.

278
00:11:18.236 --> 00:11:19.913
(SIGHS)

279
00:11:19.937 --> 00:11:22.083
It's a long story. but basically.

280
00:11:22.107 --> 00:11:25.419
a murdered woman's body
was found in the wrong grave.

281
00:11:25.443 --> 00:11:28.356
Us coming to visit is a Hail Mary.

282
00:11:28.380 --> 00:11:31.317
Wh... I don't understand.
She was murdered?

283
00:11:33.051 --> 00:11:34.395
Who was she?

284
00:11:34.419 --> 00:11:36.097
We were hoping you could tell us.

285
00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:37.765
Well. again. I...

286
00:11:37.789 --> 00:11:39.692
I wish I could. but...

287
00:11:40.493 --> 00:11:42.648
...I lose more of my memory every day.

288
00:11:43.412 --> 00:11:45.940
You know. I...
That's why I wanted to hit the road.

289
00:11:46.563 --> 00:11:50.744
just see some country
before I lose my last marble.

290
00:11:50.769 --> 00:11:52.313
Thanks anyway. Les.

291
00:11:52.337 --> 00:11:55.015
BURM: Agent Parker. isn't it? Uh...

292
00:11:55.039 --> 00:11:56.750
I sure hope it wasn't me.

293
00:11:56.774 --> 00:12:00.188
If I somehow messed up
your mom's report in any way.

294
00:12:00.212 --> 00:12:02.181
I'd never forgive myself.

295
00:12:02.860 --> 00:12:04.624
You didn't do anything wrong.

296
00:12:05.951 --> 00:12:07.229
BURM: Agent. please.

297
00:12:07.253 --> 00:12:09.798
keep me in the loop. would you?

298
00:12:09.822 --> 00:12:12.767
I mean. I won't be able
to sleep until I...

299
00:12:12.791 --> 00:12:14.469
know you got some answers.

300
00:12:14.879 --> 00:12:16.260
Will do.

301
00:12:19.532 --> 00:12:20.975
Thanks.

302
00:12:20.999 --> 00:12:22.677
No luck with Parker's old trooper.

303
00:12:22.701 --> 00:12:24.645
Guy could not remember a thing.

304
00:12:24.669 --> 00:12:26.980
Well. who didn't see that coming?

305
00:12:27.004 --> 00:12:28.438
- (PHONE CHIMING)
- Uh...

306
00:12:28.941 --> 00:12:30.451
- (GROANS)
- (EXHALES SHARPLY)

307
00:12:30.475 --> 00:12:33.654
"Military truck stolen
outside Joint Base Andrews."

308
00:12:33.678 --> 00:12:35.090
Andrews?

309
00:12:35.114 --> 00:12:36.625
Yeah. what about it?

310
00:12:36.649 --> 00:12:39.059
Well. the truck that was
picking up for the toy drive.

311
00:12:39.084 --> 00:12:41.120
I'm pretty sure that was its next stop.

312
00:12:41.687 --> 00:12:43.331
What are the chances?

313
00:12:43.355 --> 00:12:44.832
I'll call the driver.

314
00:12:44.856 --> 00:12:47.302
JIMMY: (SIGHS) Oh.
I tell you what. McGee.

315
00:12:47.326 --> 00:12:49.370
facial reconstruction is not easy.

316
00:12:49.394 --> 00:12:51.105
That intense focus?

317
00:12:51.129 --> 00:12:52.807
I mean. that really takes it out of you.

318
00:12:52.831 --> 00:12:55.177
Well. if it helps Parker. it's worth it.

319
00:12:55.201 --> 00:12:56.845
(SIGHS) I sure hope so.

320
00:12:56.869 --> 00:12:59.980
I can't handle giving any more bad news.

321
00:13:00.004 --> 00:13:02.475
- Unbelievable.
- Hmm?

322
00:13:03.408 --> 00:13:05.187
Who would steal Christmas toys?

323
00:13:05.211 --> 00:13:06.288
The Grinch.

324
00:13:06.312 --> 00:13:08.389
- Same truck?
- Yeah. same truck.

325
00:13:08.413 --> 00:13:10.491
Full of toys.
Two guys just took off with it.

326
00:13:10.515 --> 00:13:13.528
Man. is this shaping up to be
the worst Christmas ever?

327
00:13:13.552 --> 00:13:15.364
TORRES: Not yet. Jimmy.

328
00:13:15.388 --> 00:13:18.256
If the Grinch stole Christmas.
we're gonna have to steal it back.

329
00:13:26.246 --> 00:13:27.708
MCGEE: What happened with the trooper?

330
00:13:27.732 --> 00:13:30.644
Uh. the old trooper was
very sweet. and well-meaning...

331
00:13:30.668 --> 00:13:32.581
(WHISPERS): ...but not much help.

332
00:13:32.605 --> 00:13:34.448
Inept. He said it himself.

333
00:13:34.472 --> 00:13:36.717
I have more details on the stolen truck.

334
00:13:36.741 --> 00:13:39.221
Maryland PD just sent you some. uh.

335
00:13:39.245 --> 00:13:40.955
- security footage.
- (SNIFFLES)

336
00:13:40.979 --> 00:13:41.855
(KEYBOARD CLICKING)

337
00:13:41.879 --> 00:13:43.024
MCGEE: Yep. got it.

338
00:13:43.048 --> 00:13:44.359
TORRES: The truck picked up

339
00:13:44.383 --> 00:13:46.695
gifts from us and four other bases. uh.

340
00:13:46.719 --> 00:13:49.163
before double-parking
to get some fast food.

341
00:13:49.187 --> 00:13:51.155
More ineptitude.

342
00:13:52.024 --> 00:13:53.067
Look at that.

343
00:13:53.091 --> 00:13:54.503
In broad daylight.

344
00:13:54.527 --> 00:13:56.070
Well. the driver was careless.
but I doubt he thought

345
00:13:56.094 --> 00:13:58.240
anyone was gonna steal a bunch of toys.

346
00:13:58.264 --> 00:14:00.466
Yeah. well. they're not
just toys to these kids.

347
00:14:01.433 --> 00:14:03.043
Yeah. and they shouldn't
have to know that

348
00:14:03.067 --> 00:14:04.778
someone stole their holiday.

349
00:14:04.802 --> 00:14:07.048
Believe me. I know
what it's like to not have toys

350
00:14:07.072 --> 00:14:08.549
at Christmastime.

351
00:14:08.573 --> 00:14:11.186
Look. Nick. it's okay.
We'll. uh. we'll find them.

352
00:14:11.210 --> 00:14:13.654
We will. I mean. we have
BOLOs out all over the place.

353
00:14:13.678 --> 00:14:15.524
so truck's bound to show up somewhere.

354
00:14:15.548 --> 00:14:17.192
Hey. I'm not waiting for a BOLO.

355
00:14:17.216 --> 00:14:18.522
What do you have in mind?

356
00:14:18.546 --> 00:14:21.863
Well. McGee and I have
an old friend of ours who has

357
00:14:21.886 --> 00:14:24.232
all kinds of criminal connections.

358
00:14:24.256 --> 00:14:26.735
We can pay him a visit
and see what he knows.

359
00:14:26.759 --> 00:14:28.403
You know what? That's a good idea.

360
00:14:28.427 --> 00:14:30.071
Tell you what. though.
we don't have to go anywhere.

361
00:14:30.095 --> 00:14:32.740
because Sammy delivers.

362
00:14:33.105 --> 00:14:34.875
<i>SAMMY: Are you guys kidding me?</i>

363
00:14:34.899 --> 00:14:38.799
One little gambling/ racketeering
conviction ten years ago

364
00:14:38.823 --> 00:14:41.081
and you guys still consider
me some kind of Batman villain.

365
00:14:41.105 --> 00:14:43.951
Sammy. you were a murder suspect
not once but twice.

366
00:14:43.975 --> 00:14:45.720
And you were wrong both times.

367
00:14:45.744 --> 00:14:47.288
Okay. right here.

368
00:14:47.312 --> 00:14:49.591
Sammy. we're not accusing you here.

369
00:14:49.615 --> 00:14:51.426
Okay? In fact. Nick referred to you

370
00:14:51.450 --> 00:14:53.261
as an old friend before we called you.

371
00:14:53.286 --> 00:14:55.264
Nice to see how you treat old friends.

372
00:14:55.288 --> 00:14:57.765
Come on. do you know anything
about this stolen truck or not?

373
00:14:57.789 --> 00:15:01.235
Not. Look. I am way too busy
eking out a living

374
00:15:01.259 --> 00:15:03.104
to follow around truck thieves.

375
00:15:03.128 --> 00:15:05.774
And what does NCIS care
so much for anyway?

376
00:15:05.798 --> 00:15:07.908
Were they delivering your paychecks?

377
00:15:07.932 --> 00:15:09.910
- Toys.
- What?

378
00:15:09.934 --> 00:15:11.913
Hundreds of toys were stolen. Sammy.

379
00:15:11.937 --> 00:15:15.350
Ruining the holiday for. uh.
a whole lot of TAPS kids.

380
00:15:15.374 --> 00:15:17.786
TAPS kids? What's TAPS?

381
00:15:17.809 --> 00:15:20.254
Tragedy Assistance Program
for Survivors.

382
00:15:20.278 --> 00:15:23.100
It's a charity that helps
families dealing with the loss

383
00:15:23.124 --> 00:15:24.658
of a service member.

384
00:15:25.198 --> 00:15:27.729
All right. look. I don't know nothing.

385
00:15:27.753 --> 00:15:29.931
but if it's for the kids.

386
00:15:29.955 --> 00:15:33.501
maybe I make a few calls
to some former associates.

387
00:15:33.525 --> 00:15:34.735
That's all we ask.

388
00:15:34.759 --> 00:15:36.203
MCGEE: Yeah. and you tell them

389
00:15:36.227 --> 00:15:37.471
these are not just average toys. either.

390
00:15:37.495 --> 00:15:39.374
Okay? We have five Blaze the Dragons

391
00:15:39.398 --> 00:15:41.109
donated from NCIS alone.

392
00:15:41.133 --> 00:15:43.345
Blaze? I have been looking for weeks

393
00:15:43.369 --> 00:15:44.778
for that little red lizard.

394
00:15:44.802 --> 00:15:46.014
My niece would kill for it.

395
00:15:46.038 --> 00:15:47.548
I'm kidding. She's eight.

396
00:15:47.572 --> 00:15:49.783
Uh. y-you know. we can't make
any promises. but...

397
00:15:49.807 --> 00:15:50.951
Hey. maybe we can.

398
00:15:51.323 --> 00:15:53.322
Sammy. you help us find this truck.

399
00:15:53.346 --> 00:15:55.456
I'll let you have my dragon.

400
00:15:55.480 --> 00:15:56.790
I promise.

401
00:15:57.139 --> 00:15:58.607
I. uh...

402
00:15:59.216 --> 00:16:00.217
...get a pinky swear on that?

403
00:16:00.241 --> 00:16:01.498
Come on. man. just call your associates.

404
00:16:01.522 --> 00:16:03.788
Okay. fine. Enjoy your lunch.

405
00:16:06.573 --> 00:16:09.703
JIMMY: Got a face of the mystery
body from your mother's grave.

406
00:16:09.727 --> 00:16:11.215
Not bad at all. you guys.

407
00:16:11.239 --> 00:16:13.307
JIMMY: Well. you know.
it lacks a little bit of detail.

408
00:16:13.331 --> 00:16:14.543
but it might be enough
to start running through

409
00:16:14.567 --> 00:16:15.543
facial recognition.

410
00:16:15.567 --> 00:16:18.211
Mind you. finding a match
among the faces from 50 years ago

411
00:16:18.235 --> 00:16:20.081
won't be easy. but it's worth a shot.

412
00:16:20.105 --> 00:16:22.182
I got to say. Jimmy.
this is almost as good

413
00:16:22.206 --> 00:16:24.184
as that sketch
you made for me last year.

414
00:16:24.208 --> 00:16:26.187
Well. we're not finished yet.
I-I just wish I knew

415
00:16:26.211 --> 00:16:27.655
her eye color.

416
00:16:27.679 --> 00:16:29.090
You know. if we had that.
it would really help

417
00:16:29.114 --> 00:16:30.191
with facial rec.

418
00:16:30.722 --> 00:16:32.249
Try green.

419
00:16:33.085 --> 00:16:35.096
Why green? Do you know her?

420
00:16:35.120 --> 00:16:37.164
No. but call me crazy.

421
00:16:37.188 --> 00:16:39.967
she kind of reminds me
of the retired trooper's wife.

422
00:16:39.991 --> 00:16:41.669
You know. the one in the photo.

423
00:16:41.693 --> 00:16:43.705
"The prettiest green eyes."

424
00:16:43.729 --> 00:16:46.375
- They could be related.
- Similar face. same features?

425
00:16:46.399 --> 00:16:48.342
Eh. you never know. Anything's possible.

426
00:16:48.366 --> 00:16:50.345
We've all learned that today.

427
00:16:50.369 --> 00:16:51.879
All right. we need to take a closer look

428
00:16:51.903 --> 00:16:53.615
at the old trooper and his wife.

429
00:16:53.639 --> 00:16:55.006
You never know.

430
00:17:00.144 --> 00:17:02.856
- Did you see that?
- I saw that.

431
00:17:02.880 --> 00:17:04.893
- Hope.
- Mm-hmm.

432
00:17:04.916 --> 00:17:06.428
Tell you what. man. this is good.

433
00:17:06.451 --> 00:17:07.695
I could eat another one
of these. Very tasty.

434
00:17:07.720 --> 00:17:08.896
Oh. yeah.

435
00:17:08.921 --> 00:17:10.565
Right? Who knew. right? That Sammy. uh.

436
00:17:10.588 --> 00:17:13.300
who was a crook.
could be such a good cook?

437
00:17:13.325 --> 00:17:14.536
SAMMY: I heard that.

438
00:17:14.559 --> 00:17:16.203
Frankly. I'm a little more insulted

439
00:17:16.228 --> 00:17:18.271
that you would doubt my culinary skills.

440
00:17:18.296 --> 00:17:20.107
And I'm beginning to think you're. uh.

441
00:17:20.132 --> 00:17:22.009
a bit too easily offended there. Sammy.

442
00:17:22.034 --> 00:17:23.545
Any luck with your calls?

443
00:17:23.568 --> 00:17:26.538
Yeah. I might even have a lead for you.

444
00:17:27.305 --> 00:17:29.784
But it's. uh. it's complicated.

445
00:17:29.807 --> 00:17:32.421
- How complicated?
- Word on the street is.

446
00:17:32.444 --> 00:17:34.055
my associates may know a guy

447
00:17:34.078 --> 00:17:36.289
who might know
a couple of other guys that

448
00:17:36.314 --> 00:17:38.259
maybe were looking to...

449
00:17:38.282 --> 00:17:40.662
steal a truckload of military equipment.

450
00:17:40.685 --> 00:17:43.597
So. any word on. uh. who these
guys are or where they might be?

451
00:17:43.622 --> 00:17:45.733
Eh. you see. that's where
it gets complicated.

452
00:17:45.758 --> 00:17:47.300
Again. how complicated?

453
00:17:47.325 --> 00:17:49.871
Okay. One of the thieves
might be the son

454
00:17:49.894 --> 00:17:51.772
of a very scary man who has the ability

455
00:17:51.797 --> 00:17:53.942
to hurt any law-abiding sloppy joe maker

456
00:17:53.965 --> 00:17:56.510
who happens to help the feds
put his kid away.

457
00:17:56.535 --> 00:18:00.548
We can protect you from this
"very scary man." if necessary.

458
00:18:00.573 --> 00:18:02.182
You're adorable. you know that?

459
00:18:02.207 --> 00:18:05.118
I tell you what. Sammy.
you call your people back

460
00:18:05.143 --> 00:18:06.654
and you tell them you have two buyers

461
00:18:06.679 --> 00:18:08.957
that are willing to pay
good cash for that truck.

462
00:18:08.980 --> 00:18:10.791
Two buyer... you two?

463
00:18:10.816 --> 00:18:12.483
Just make the call. all right?

464
00:18:12.991 --> 00:18:15.327
All right. don't forget.
I want that dragon.

465
00:18:15.353 --> 00:18:16.798
- Come on.
- Would you please call? Come on.

466
00:18:16.821 --> 00:18:18.266
All right. all right. Fine.

467
00:18:18.289 --> 00:18:19.601
He said "please."

468
00:18:20.176 --> 00:18:22.167
The hostility doesn't help.

469
00:18:25.430 --> 00:18:28.742
Okay. so. Illinois State Police
records confirm

470
00:18:28.767 --> 00:18:31.345
that Trooper Burm's wife Donna
did indeed pass away

471
00:18:31.368 --> 00:18:32.713
from cancer three years ago.

472
00:18:32.738 --> 00:18:35.148
And nothing about Donna having a secret

473
00:18:35.173 --> 00:18:40.054
look-alike cousin or sister
that died 50 years earlier?

474
00:18:40.077 --> 00:18:41.955
Oh. give me a break.
This is practically microfilm

475
00:18:41.980 --> 00:18:43.156
we're dealing with here.

476
00:18:43.181 --> 00:18:44.392
Yeah. I'm kind of glad we are.

477
00:18:44.415 --> 00:18:46.317
Check out this shot I just found.

478
00:18:46.884 --> 00:18:49.798
Burm's retirement party from 2006.

479
00:18:49.822 --> 00:18:51.231
Still a cute couple.

480
00:18:51.256 --> 00:18:53.768
Yeah. but does she look anything like

481
00:18:53.791 --> 00:18:56.203
the photo in Lester's RV?

482
00:18:56.228 --> 00:18:57.638
Look at the eye color.

483
00:18:57.663 --> 00:18:59.140
KNIGHT: They're definitely not green.

484
00:18:59.163 --> 00:19:01.066
Maybe he got remarried?

485
00:19:01.731 --> 00:19:03.344
Hey. guys.

486
00:19:03.536 --> 00:19:05.179
How's everything?

487
00:19:05.202 --> 00:19:07.672
Fine. Why?

488
00:19:08.213 --> 00:19:09.651
PARKER: I know that look.

489
00:19:09.674 --> 00:19:11.986
Are you really about
to give us more bad news?

490
00:19:12.009 --> 00:19:13.186
(LAUGHS): No! No.

491
00:19:13.211 --> 00:19:14.855
- It's not bad. Per se.
- No. No.

492
00:19:14.880 --> 00:19:16.657
- It's more like... Uh...
- It's weird.

493
00:19:16.682 --> 00:19:18.192
- It is. it is weird.
- It is weird. Yeah. yeah.

494
00:19:18.215 --> 00:19:19.627
No. it's not weird.
It's a... no. it's. uh.

495
00:19:19.652 --> 00:19:20.494
it's more of a...
it's like a breakthrough.

496
00:19:20.519 --> 00:19:21.996
Please just say it.

497
00:19:22.019 --> 00:19:23.297
Well. remember the lipstick

498
00:19:23.320 --> 00:19:24.999
that was on the wine glass we retrieved

499
00:19:25.022 --> 00:19:27.759
from your place the night
that your father was killed?

500
00:19:31.696 --> 00:19:33.207
Still no DNA match?

501
00:19:33.230 --> 00:19:35.509
We did actually find a match. finally.

502
00:19:35.534 --> 00:19:37.010
Finally. Exactly.

503
00:19:37.035 --> 00:19:38.278
Who is it?

504
00:19:38.302 --> 00:19:40.213
- We do not have a name. But...
- But...

505
00:19:40.238 --> 00:19:43.917
But she appears to be
a 48% genetic match

506
00:19:43.942 --> 00:19:47.188
to the mystery woman down in autopsy.

507
00:19:47.211 --> 00:19:48.322
(SCOFFS)

508
00:19:48.346 --> 00:19:51.224
One more time. Slowly.

509
00:19:51.249 --> 00:19:55.195
Whoever had wine with your dad
the night that he was murdered

510
00:19:55.220 --> 00:19:58.967
is closely related
to the woman we exhumed

511
00:19:58.990 --> 00:20:01.192
from your mother's grave.

512
00:20:14.538 --> 00:20:15.874
(PARKER EXHALES)

513
00:20:17.115 --> 00:20:19.086
Who is she?

514
00:20:19.111 --> 00:20:20.478
Do you know?

515
00:20:27.251 --> 00:20:28.630
Oh...

516
00:20:28.961 --> 00:20:30.664
How long's he been here?

517
00:20:30.689 --> 00:20:32.834
Since before I got here.

518
00:20:32.857 --> 00:20:35.769
He's not moving much. just muttering.

519
00:20:36.009 --> 00:20:37.927
Lots of muttering.

520
00:20:38.663 --> 00:20:40.074
Let me get this straight.

521
00:20:40.097 --> 00:20:42.977
The mystery woman
in Parker's mother's grave

522
00:20:43.000 --> 00:20:45.246
is related to our lipstick woman?

523
00:20:45.269 --> 00:20:47.981
Yeah. and we think Parker's dad
may have known both.

524
00:20:48.006 --> 00:20:49.482
Along with a lot of other stuff

525
00:20:49.507 --> 00:20:51.819
that Parker's dad isn't here to explain.

526
00:20:51.844 --> 00:20:53.453
I can hear you.

527
00:20:53.478 --> 00:20:56.089
Uh. hey.

528
00:20:56.114 --> 00:20:57.424
Morning. Parker.

529
00:20:57.449 --> 00:20:59.426
MCGEE: You. uh.
you been here all night or...?

530
00:20:59.451 --> 00:21:02.296
Nope. nope. I. uh.
I couldn't sleep at home.

531
00:21:02.319 --> 00:21:06.099
so I. uh. came in to wait for a miracle.

532
00:21:06.124 --> 00:21:07.935
Isn't that what's supposed to happen

533
00:21:07.960 --> 00:21:10.739
this time of year? A little
holiday magic in the air?

534
00:21:10.762 --> 00:21:13.775
Silver bells and joy
to the world and all of that?

535
00:21:13.798 --> 00:21:15.834
(PHONE CHIMING)

536
00:21:16.567 --> 00:21:20.147
It's Nick. Sloppy Joe Sammy
wants to meet us in Baltimore.

537
00:21:20.172 --> 00:21:21.782
Hmm. Maybe he found the toy thieves.

538
00:21:21.807 --> 00:21:24.484
Um. hate to leave you
like this. Parker. but...

539
00:21:24.509 --> 00:21:26.621
What. are you kidding?
I mean. if I can't get

540
00:21:26.644 --> 00:21:29.156
a Christmas miracle.
maybe you can. Hell. go ahead.

541
00:21:29.181 --> 00:21:31.826
Go. Go and find the truck. Go ahead.

542
00:21:31.849 --> 00:21:34.651
This is unbelievable.

543
00:21:34.674 --> 00:21:36.664
Uh. you know what? I'll watch facial rec

544
00:21:36.689 --> 00:21:38.465
for you. Parker. I promise.

545
00:21:38.490 --> 00:21:41.301
And how about you and I.
we go get some coffee?

546
00:21:41.326 --> 00:21:42.961
- Mm-hmm.
- (SIGHS)

547
00:21:46.564 --> 00:21:49.467
- Thank you.
- Okay. (CLEARS THROAT)

548
00:21:52.086 --> 00:21:54.281
- I'm sorry. Jess.
- Sorry for what?

549
00:21:54.306 --> 00:21:56.250
For losing it down at Kasie's.

550
00:21:56.273 --> 00:21:58.086
Oh. are you serious?
After all the curveballs that

551
00:21:58.109 --> 00:22:00.188
have been thrown at you? I'm
surprised you're still standing.

552
00:22:00.211 --> 00:22:02.323
You know. the one thing that I
haven't allowed myself to think.

553
00:22:02.346 --> 00:22:04.157
much less say out loud. until now.

554
00:22:04.182 --> 00:22:06.527
is the possibility that my mother

555
00:22:06.550 --> 00:22:09.329
- might somehow still be alive.
- Mm.

556
00:22:09.354 --> 00:22:11.565
Yeah. I was wondering
if all of this. uh.

557
00:22:11.588 --> 00:22:13.334
had you wondering.

558
00:22:13.357 --> 00:22:15.192
And if she is.

559
00:22:15.728 --> 00:22:17.112
I'd be...

560
00:22:18.130 --> 00:22:20.842
happy. I guess?

561
00:22:20.865 --> 00:22:23.544
Yeah. of course. Why wouldn't you be?

562
00:22:23.867 --> 00:22:26.881
Because first. I would need to hear

563
00:22:26.904 --> 00:22:30.218
a really good reason
why she ran out on her family.

564
00:22:30.241 --> 00:22:33.010
her own kids. and never came back.

565
00:22:34.078 --> 00:22:35.447
(PHONE CHIMES)

566
00:22:36.681 --> 00:22:39.027
- Oh. wow. That was quick.
- What is that?

567
00:22:39.050 --> 00:22:41.394
I emailed the Illinois State
Police Archives

568
00:22:41.419 --> 00:22:43.530
late last night.
and they just got back to me.

569
00:22:43.555 --> 00:22:45.199
What were you looking for?

570
00:22:45.222 --> 00:22:48.536
Trooper Burm's record had Donna
listed as his one and only wife.

571
00:22:48.559 --> 00:22:52.073
so I wanted to get another look
at that photo from his house

572
00:22:52.096 --> 00:22:54.742
to make sure it was the same Donna.

573
00:22:54.767 --> 00:22:56.243
And the archives had it?

574
00:22:56.268 --> 00:22:58.712
"We believe we found
the exact photo you described

575
00:22:58.737 --> 00:23:02.507
from Trooper Burm's
1971 promotion ceremony."

576
00:23:04.209 --> 00:23:05.385
Oh. my God.

577
00:23:05.410 --> 00:23:07.555
Don't tell me. another curveball?

578
00:23:07.952 --> 00:23:09.846
More like a fastball to the head.

579
00:23:11.615 --> 00:23:13.718
It's the girl from the crash.

580
00:23:17.923 --> 00:23:20.500
Hey. there. Agents. Come on in.

581
00:23:20.525 --> 00:23:23.436
Hoping this means you got some answers.

582
00:23:23.461 --> 00:23:26.307
No. just a few follow-up questions.

583
00:23:26.330 --> 00:23:29.609
Uh-oh. Whenever I said that on the job.

584
00:23:29.634 --> 00:23:31.011
it was more than that.

585
00:23:31.036 --> 00:23:32.846
Oh. not this. If anything.

586
00:23:32.871 --> 00:23:34.481
we're actually embarrassed.
we still need help.

587
00:23:34.506 --> 00:23:38.675
Oh. well. d-don't be.
I mean. I'm your man.

588
00:23:39.329 --> 00:23:41.266
Oh. but as a bonus...

589
00:23:42.780 --> 00:23:45.659
I get to look at this lovely
photo of you and Donna again.

590
00:23:45.682 --> 00:23:46.961
- (BURM CHUCKLES)
- What year was this?

591
00:23:46.984 --> 00:23:48.997
Ah. that was the fall of '71.

592
00:23:49.020 --> 00:23:51.965
and I don't recall telling you her name.

593
00:23:51.990 --> 00:23:54.535
Bad memory. Yeah. we know.

594
00:23:54.558 --> 00:23:56.003
Is this not Donna?

595
00:23:56.028 --> 00:23:59.440
Since you clearly done a
complete background check on me.

596
00:23:59.463 --> 00:24:02.509
for whatever reason. you already
know that's my first wife.

597
00:24:02.534 --> 00:24:05.212
So why leave that detail out yesterday?

598
00:24:05.237 --> 00:24:06.680
Well. yesterday. I didn't know

599
00:24:06.703 --> 00:24:08.516
I was being questioned under oath.

600
00:24:08.539 --> 00:24:10.317
It's just that there's no mention

601
00:24:10.342 --> 00:24:12.720
of a first wife in your records.

602
00:24:12.743 --> 00:24:16.924
Judy and I married way too young. and

603
00:24:16.949 --> 00:24:18.826
one day. she up and left me. and I

604
00:24:18.851 --> 00:24:21.061
couldn't track her. which is not to say

605
00:24:21.086 --> 00:24:22.830
I don't miss her and Donna.

606
00:24:22.855 --> 00:24:24.597
Any kids with Judy?

607
00:24:24.622 --> 00:24:27.468
We're on the same side. What's
with all the questions here?

608
00:24:27.491 --> 00:24:29.160
Well. you said. uh...

609
00:24:29.862 --> 00:24:31.997
you didn't know this little girl.

610
00:24:33.998 --> 00:24:36.044
KNIGHT: But according to that.

611
00:24:36.067 --> 00:24:38.645
and the. uh. part
that you cropped out...

612
00:24:38.670 --> 00:24:41.138
You knew Lily very well.

613
00:24:42.212 --> 00:24:43.548
Okay.

614
00:24:45.309 --> 00:24:46.677
"Okay" what?

615
00:24:47.679 --> 00:24:51.682
Her name's not Lily anymore. It's Nelly.

616
00:24:52.115 --> 00:24:53.926
Least it was the last I heard.

617
00:24:53.951 --> 00:24:55.429
but she. uh...

618
00:24:55.552 --> 00:24:56.897
(SIGHS)

619
00:24:56.922 --> 00:24:59.400
...she's probably changed it
again by now.

620
00:24:59.702 --> 00:25:01.275
Your daughter?

621
00:25:02.660 --> 00:25:04.605
Judy took her with her when she left.

622
00:25:04.628 --> 00:25:07.240
and I looked for her for years.

623
00:25:07.265 --> 00:25:10.211
till I met Donna and I moved on.

624
00:25:10.234 --> 00:25:12.846
- Or tried to.
- Do you know where she is?

625
00:25:12.871 --> 00:25:14.873
(SIGHS) No.

626
00:25:15.772 --> 00:25:18.086
The older I get. the more I want to.

627
00:25:18.109 --> 00:25:21.588
I mean. I wasn't much of a father or...

628
00:25:21.613 --> 00:25:25.717
a husband. but there are things
that I would love to clear up.

629
00:25:26.284 --> 00:25:28.619
Fences I'd love to mend.

630
00:25:30.386 --> 00:25:33.567
Anything else that you lied about?

631
00:25:33.590 --> 00:25:34.935
I didn't lie to you.

632
00:25:34.960 --> 00:25:38.638
Just didn't offer it up.
It's nobody else's business.

633
00:25:38.663 --> 00:25:40.673
Did you know my mother?

634
00:25:40.698 --> 00:25:42.809
Eleanor Parker?

635
00:25:42.834 --> 00:25:45.078
I wish I did. son. believe me.

636
00:25:45.103 --> 00:25:48.382
I would love to be able
to help you. I really would.

637
00:25:48.405 --> 00:25:50.307
(BURM SIGHS)

638
00:25:50.824 --> 00:25:52.953
KNIGHT: And no memory of the car crash?

639
00:25:52.978 --> 00:25:54.387
Even with Lily standing there?

640
00:25:54.412 --> 00:25:56.723
Well. that was nothing special.

641
00:25:56.748 --> 00:26:00.994
Taking Lily on my calls was one
of the reasons Judy left me.

642
00:26:01.019 --> 00:26:04.765
Do you know what last name
Lily or Nelly might be using?

643
00:26:04.788 --> 00:26:06.834
Well. if I did.
I would've found her by now.

644
00:26:06.857 --> 00:26:10.939
and if you people find her.
please let me tag along.

645
00:26:10.962 --> 00:26:13.707
Why don't you come back
to NCIS with us now.

646
00:26:13.730 --> 00:26:14.942
and we'll. uh.

647
00:26:14.967 --> 00:26:17.667
we'll run a fresh search
with this new name.

648
00:26:18.227 --> 00:26:21.214
You did mention how much
you missed the action. Les.

649
00:26:21.239 --> 00:26:23.017
I've dreamed of this day.

650
00:26:23.040 --> 00:26:25.987
In fact. I could drive us all
back in this baby right now.

651
00:26:26.010 --> 00:26:28.455
Don't lose your spot. We'll drive.

652
00:26:28.480 --> 00:26:30.882
Okay. Okay.

653
00:26:36.688 --> 00:26:38.872
Uh. 35th and Chestnut.

654
00:26:39.757 --> 00:26:41.759
Right alley. but no Sammy.

655
00:26:42.527 --> 00:26:44.572
Did you try calling him again?

656
00:26:44.596 --> 00:26:47.219
Well. I got his voicemail. like.
five times on the way here.

657
00:26:47.242 --> 00:26:48.900
Maybe he flaked.

658
00:26:50.634 --> 00:26:52.046
Or maybe not.

659
00:26:52.469 --> 00:26:54.762
TORRES: That's the
truck I loaded yesterday.

660
00:27:05.916 --> 00:27:07.194
TORRES: Oh. my God. Sammy.

661
00:27:07.219 --> 00:27:08.328
MCGEE: All right. hold on. We got you.

662
00:27:08.353 --> 00:27:09.329
TORRES: Sammy!

663
00:27:09.354 --> 00:27:11.199
- (SAMMY COUGHS)
- Oh. man.

664
00:27:11.222 --> 00:27:13.258
We're here. we're here.
we're here. We got you.

665
00:27:17.429 --> 00:27:19.373
Okay. Torres and McGee will head back

666
00:27:19.396 --> 00:27:21.142
as soon as their informant's
out of the hospital.

667
00:27:21.165 --> 00:27:22.843
He's banged up. but okay.

668
00:27:22.866 --> 00:27:25.346
When did toy thieves get so dangerous?

669
00:27:25.369 --> 00:27:26.946
- (COMPUTER BEEPS)
- Got a hit

670
00:27:26.971 --> 00:27:29.250
on maybe our longest shot ever.

671
00:27:29.273 --> 00:27:30.851
but "Never. never give in."

672
00:27:30.875 --> 00:27:32.552
We're quoting Churchill now?

673
00:27:32.576 --> 00:27:33.886
Whatever works.

674
00:27:33.911 --> 00:27:36.723
Um. so I added the names Nell and Nelly

675
00:27:36.748 --> 00:27:39.740
to our DNA search for both our
lipstick and our mystery woman.

676
00:27:39.763 --> 00:27:43.230
and got a 48% match to a
Delaware school teacher.

677
00:27:43.255 --> 00:27:45.717
who took a home ancestry test
two years ago.

678
00:27:45.740 --> 00:27:47.101
Nelly is that teacher?

679
00:27:47.125 --> 00:27:48.568
No. not her. Her mother.

680
00:27:48.593 --> 00:27:50.894
Penelope Johnson of Wilmington.

681
00:27:51.828 --> 00:27:55.400
Nelly. as in "Pe-NEL-ope."
Get it? Nelly?

682
00:27:56.468 --> 00:27:58.912
Wow. that is a very long shot.

683
00:27:58.935 --> 00:28:00.614
Except for one thing.

684
00:28:00.637 --> 00:28:02.782
My mother's maiden name was Johnson.

685
00:28:02.807 --> 00:28:05.653
- Got to be a connection.
- Oh. I just got chills.

686
00:28:05.676 --> 00:28:08.221
And you think that's worth the
two-hour drive to Wilmington?

687
00:28:08.246 --> 00:28:09.909
Well. if you're too tired. I understand.

688
00:28:09.932 --> 00:28:10.990
Oh. no. I'm not saying that.

689
00:28:11.015 --> 00:28:13.094
I'm just saying. we have
an old state trooper on ice

690
00:28:13.117 --> 00:28:14.428
in the conference room.

691
00:28:14.451 --> 00:28:16.430
We're not really gonna
bring him with us.

692
00:28:16.453 --> 00:28:17.730
You trust him?

693
00:28:17.755 --> 00:28:19.900
I don't know how I feel about him.

694
00:28:19.923 --> 00:28:22.069
Neither do I. Let him wait.

695
00:28:22.094 --> 00:28:24.404
Besides. this is my white whale.
not his.

696
00:28:24.429 --> 00:28:26.663
I'll send you the address. Ahab.

697
00:28:31.001 --> 00:28:32.779
This better get me two Blazes.

698
00:28:32.804 --> 00:28:34.214
Sammy. we are so sorry.

699
00:28:34.239 --> 00:28:36.084
Yeah. man. we shouldn't have pushed you.

700
00:28:36.107 --> 00:28:37.684
MCGEE: Who did this?

701
00:28:37.709 --> 00:28:39.952
Was it the very scary man
that you were worried about?

702
00:28:39.977 --> 00:28:43.090
No. it was his creepy kid
and some other guy.

703
00:28:43.115 --> 00:28:45.093
I'll tell you. though. mentioning NCIS

704
00:28:45.116 --> 00:28:46.894
might've been
the only thing that saved me.

705
00:28:46.917 --> 00:28:50.431
Well. NCIS is done playing.
Tell us how to find this guy.

706
00:28:50.454 --> 00:28:52.500
Why? So he can kill me next time?

707
00:28:52.523 --> 00:28:54.434
Oh. he needs to know what his son did.

708
00:28:54.459 --> 00:28:55.635
He won't care.

709
00:28:55.660 --> 00:28:58.105
Well. we'll make him care. believe me.

710
00:28:58.130 --> 00:29:01.008
No. you won't. I'm done. No more.

711
00:29:01.031 --> 00:29:02.343
- I'm done.
- Sammy...

712
00:29:02.366 --> 00:29:04.278
- Sammy. come on.
- SAMMY: No more. I'm done.

713
00:29:04.301 --> 00:29:06.837
I'll get my own ride home.

714
00:29:07.971 --> 00:29:09.517
(SIGHS)

715
00:29:09.540 --> 00:29:11.184
Probably should've asked before we left.

716
00:29:11.209 --> 00:29:14.387
but. uh. why didn't we just call her?

717
00:29:14.412 --> 00:29:17.125
Uh. to ask her what? "Uh. by any chance

718
00:29:17.148 --> 00:29:19.492
"are you related to the. uh.

719
00:29:19.517 --> 00:29:22.563
dead lady buried in my mother's grave?"

720
00:29:22.586 --> 00:29:25.288
Yeah. it's. uh. it's better in person.

721
00:29:25.923 --> 00:29:28.801
And here we are.

722
00:29:29.375 --> 00:29:30.837
(SIGHS) You ready?

723
00:29:30.862 --> 00:29:32.380
Are you ready?

724
00:29:33.029 --> 00:29:34.463
Hell no.

725
00:29:38.715 --> 00:29:40.095
Hello.

726
00:29:43.607 --> 00:29:45.057
KNIGHT: Hi.

727
00:29:46.943 --> 00:29:50.824
Uh. we are here looking for
Nell or Nelly Johnson?

728
00:29:50.847 --> 00:29:53.817
Grandma. it's somebody
selling something.

729
00:29:54.451 --> 00:29:56.778
Don't make assumptions. Eleanor.

730
00:29:58.515 --> 00:29:59.692
Eleanor?

731
00:29:59.717 --> 00:30:01.694
NELL: Old-school name for an old soul.

732
00:30:01.925 --> 00:30:03.961
Now. what can I help you with?

733
00:30:04.862 --> 00:30:06.173
We're sorry to bother you so late.

734
00:30:06.198 --> 00:30:08.541
but I'm Agent Knight
and this is Agent Parker.

735
00:30:08.566 --> 00:30:11.269
And we're here to ask you a few. uh...

736
00:30:13.823 --> 00:30:15.125
...questions.

737
00:30:20.730 --> 00:30:22.054
Lily?

738
00:30:24.413 --> 00:30:25.981
Aldie?

739
00:30:35.125 --> 00:30:36.836
It was just a few months. Aldie.

740
00:30:36.861 --> 00:30:39.539
We would play outside while your mother.

741
00:30:39.564 --> 00:30:43.109
Eleanor. gave piano lessons
to my mom. Judy.

742
00:30:43.134 --> 00:30:45.212
Why do I have no memory of that?

743
00:30:45.237 --> 00:30:47.079
I'm no psychologist. but the reason

744
00:30:47.104 --> 00:30:48.382
we stopped having play dates

745
00:30:48.405 --> 00:30:49.782
might have something to do with it.

746
00:30:49.807 --> 00:30:51.751
No more piano lessons?

747
00:30:51.776 --> 00:30:53.221
NELL: Well. let's see...

748
00:30:53.244 --> 00:30:57.548
It started the day
that I told you a secret.

749
00:30:58.682 --> 00:31:00.794
That my mother was being abused

750
00:31:00.817 --> 00:31:02.395
by my father.

751
00:31:02.420 --> 00:31:04.832
Trooper Sergeant Lester Burm.

752
00:31:04.855 --> 00:31:06.433
Yeah. Lester.

753
00:31:06.950 --> 00:31:09.853
I made you promise me. Aldie.

754
00:31:11.296 --> 00:31:12.573
<i>I remember practically begging.</i>

755
00:31:12.596 --> 00:31:14.566
<i>"Please. you can't tell anyone."</i>

756
00:31:15.366 --> 00:31:16.410
And you didn't.

757
00:31:16.433 --> 00:31:20.087
You didn't have to. because
my mom told yours herself.

758
00:31:20.438 --> 00:31:22.316
which started them on a mission.

759
00:31:22.339 --> 00:31:24.285
And you couldn't go to the police?

760
00:31:24.308 --> 00:31:26.686
Lester was the police.

761
00:31:26.711 --> 00:31:28.221
Times were different then.

762
00:31:28.703 --> 00:31:31.190
And your mom never
told your dad. either.

763
00:31:31.214 --> 00:31:33.361
'cause she didn't want him
to talk her out of it.

764
00:31:33.384 --> 00:31:34.827
Talk her out of what?

765
00:31:34.852 --> 00:31:38.298
Their plan was to hide us in Pittsburgh.

766
00:31:38.323 --> 00:31:40.924
at the home of your mom's
second cousin. Connie.

767
00:31:41.674 --> 00:31:43.468
But then came that day.

768
00:31:44.260 --> 00:31:46.807
<i>That one day when they were
finalizing their plans</i>

769
00:31:46.830 --> 00:31:48.141
<i>over a bottle of wine.</i>

770
00:31:48.164 --> 00:31:50.133
<i>and Lester walked in.</i>

771
00:31:50.517 --> 00:31:51.911
<i>He just lost it.</i>

772
00:31:51.935 --> 00:31:53.747
<i>Like always.</i>

773
00:31:53.770 --> 00:31:55.982
Your mom drove you home.

774
00:31:56.007 --> 00:31:58.286
but then she came back to help us.

775
00:31:58.309 --> 00:32:01.489
only to find that Lester
had taken me to his job.

776
00:32:01.512 --> 00:32:04.124
So. our mothers hopped in your mom's car

777
00:32:04.147 --> 00:32:06.083
and took off to come and get me.

778
00:32:06.983 --> 00:32:09.497
- But they didn't get far.
- The crash.

779
00:32:09.520 --> 00:32:11.964
Lester forced them off the road.

780
00:32:11.989 --> 00:32:14.201
Your mom somehow got away.

781
00:32:14.224 --> 00:32:15.709
but not mine.

782
00:32:16.361 --> 00:32:17.878
She was hurt.

783
00:32:18.363 --> 00:32:21.173
Lester... finished the job.

784
00:32:21.198 --> 00:32:24.010
And after that. he just came
back to me at the station

785
00:32:24.035 --> 00:32:26.036
like nothing ever happened.

786
00:32:26.761 --> 00:32:30.942
waiting for someone to call in
about the "tragic accident."

787
00:32:33.345 --> 00:32:34.822
KNIGHT: This accident?

788
00:32:34.846 --> 00:32:35.989
NELL: Oh...

789
00:32:36.012 --> 00:32:38.583
Oh. my God. That's the night.

790
00:32:39.517 --> 00:32:41.852
I didn't know it made the papers.

791
00:32:42.359 --> 00:32:45.031
I was terrified of Lester before.

792
00:32:45.056 --> 00:32:47.166
but watching him cover his tracks...

793
00:32:47.191 --> 00:32:50.337
He let everyone think that
Eleanor was drunk driving.

794
00:32:50.362 --> 00:32:51.796
but it was him.

795
00:32:54.665 --> 00:32:56.500
He killed your mother.

796
00:32:58.234 --> 00:33:00.171
And then. he went after yours.

797
00:33:02.239 --> 00:33:04.352
While Lester took my mom's body
to the morgue.

798
00:33:04.375 --> 00:33:07.153
your mom somehow circled back for me.

799
00:33:07.178 --> 00:33:09.390
and we took off for Pittsburgh.

800
00:33:09.413 --> 00:33:11.592
Obviously. you made it.

801
00:33:11.615 --> 00:33:13.160
We nearly both did.

802
00:33:13.183 --> 00:33:16.435
<i>Made it all the way
to a motel in Toledo.</i>

803
00:33:17.269 --> 00:33:19.231
Your mom couldn't bear

804
00:33:19.256 --> 00:33:21.868
to let her family think she was dead.

805
00:33:21.893 --> 00:33:24.037
She couldn't wait
to get back to you. Aldie.

806
00:33:24.060 --> 00:33:26.823
<i>Not till she knew
that I was safe.</i>

807
00:33:27.398 --> 00:33:28.949
<i>Then he found us.</i>

808
00:33:33.037 --> 00:33:35.472
You know those things
you wish you could unsee?

809
00:33:36.026 --> 00:33:37.790
<i>For me. that was him...</i>

810
00:33:38.828 --> 00:33:40.626
<i>dragging her out.</i>

811
00:33:42.680 --> 00:33:44.882
That was the last time
I ever saw your mom.

812
00:33:47.698 --> 00:33:49.342
He killed her.

813
00:33:49.802 --> 00:33:51.954
I'm so sorry. Aldie.

814
00:33:53.758 --> 00:33:57.438
Cousin Connie found me
the next day. and I have been

815
00:33:57.461 --> 00:34:00.565
hiding from that man ever since.

816
00:34:03.034 --> 00:34:04.317
Aldie...

817
00:34:04.868 --> 00:34:08.014
your mother sacrificed
everything for me.

818
00:34:08.039 --> 00:34:10.250
Helping others was just in her nature.

819
00:34:10.273 --> 00:34:12.119
which. according to Roman.

820
00:34:12.143 --> 00:34:14.755
is the nature that she passed onto you.

821
00:34:14.778 --> 00:34:17.123
Roman. You went to see him.

822
00:34:17.148 --> 00:34:20.393
Somehow. he found Connie's
number in an old phone book.

823
00:34:20.418 --> 00:34:22.295
Called out of the blue and said.

824
00:34:22.318 --> 00:34:24.097
"Do you know any Lilys?"

825
00:34:24.121 --> 00:34:25.690
Connie said no.

826
00:34:26.342 --> 00:34:27.610
But that's when I knew

827
00:34:27.635 --> 00:34:30.070
it was way past time
to pay Roman a visit.

828
00:34:31.394 --> 00:34:33.606
In fact. I'm surprised
you don't already know this.

829
00:34:33.630 --> 00:34:35.943
because when I told him
the whole story that night.

830
00:34:35.967 --> 00:34:37.726
he couldn't wait to tell you.

831
00:34:40.237 --> 00:34:42.206
He never got the chance.

832
00:34:46.577 --> 00:34:49.914
I hope my visit didn't draw
Lester's attention.

833
00:34:51.782 --> 00:34:54.494
He may be old. but if he is out there.

834
00:34:54.518 --> 00:34:57.554
I still wouldn't put anything past him.

835
00:35:02.760 --> 00:35:05.137
Eh. I hate to complain. Agent Parker.

836
00:35:05.161 --> 00:35:07.840
but I would've preferred
sitting in the conference room.

837
00:35:07.864 --> 00:35:08.876
(CHUCKLES)

838
00:35:08.900 --> 00:35:10.092
(PARKER SIGHS)

839
00:35:10.467 --> 00:35:11.978
Still no memory of this?

840
00:35:12.003 --> 00:35:13.349
BURM: What's the matter?

841
00:35:13.373 --> 00:35:16.849
I mean. I already told you.
this was such a long time ago.

842
00:35:16.873 --> 00:35:18.552
That's a mighty big flashlight.

843
00:35:18.576 --> 00:35:22.021
Big enough to crush Judy's skull
after you ran her off the road.

844
00:35:22.045 --> 00:35:24.492
(LAUGHING) What? That's crazy. You...

845
00:35:24.516 --> 00:35:26.159
You found Lily.

846
00:35:26.182 --> 00:35:29.096
What we found is your first wife's body

847
00:35:29.119 --> 00:35:30.521
in my mother's grave.

848
00:35:30.545 --> 00:35:32.532
Now. you promised me
that if you found her...

849
00:35:32.556 --> 00:35:34.768
Is that flashlight the way
you killed my mother. too?

850
00:35:34.791 --> 00:35:36.704
After you dragged her out of the motel?

851
00:35:36.728 --> 00:35:38.371
Just talking nonsense now. Just...

852
00:35:38.394 --> 00:35:40.007
That's why you're driving around.

853
00:35:40.030 --> 00:35:43.043
You're not seeing the country.
you are looking for her.

854
00:35:43.067 --> 00:35:44.376
I've been looking all my life.

855
00:35:44.400 --> 00:35:46.346
Well. too late. Lester. We found her.

856
00:35:46.369 --> 00:35:48.114
Lily's doing just fine.

857
00:35:48.139 --> 00:35:50.659
She's just great without you.
thanks to my mother.

858
00:35:50.684 --> 00:35:52.385
Eleanor Parker.

859
00:35:52.409 --> 00:35:54.922
You know that name. So where is she?

860
00:35:54.945 --> 00:35:56.456
You put Lily's mother

861
00:35:56.480 --> 00:35:59.259
in Eleanor's grave.
so where did you put Eleanor?

862
00:35:59.282 --> 00:36:00.827
Where?

863
00:36:00.851 --> 00:36:05.322
Son. I wish I knew what you were
talking about. but I swear...

864
00:36:09.264 --> 00:36:11.134
<i>(SIGHS)</i>

865
00:36:16.414 --> 00:36:17.882
Let's go.

866
00:36:25.141 --> 00:36:27.501
You say that you have a bad memory.

867
00:36:29.628 --> 00:36:33.025
We're gonna correct that. right now.

868
00:36:33.800 --> 00:36:35.996
<i>TORRES: Oh. man.
the TAPS Christmas party</i>

869
00:36:36.019 --> 00:36:37.396
- Kids are already here.
- Yeah.

870
00:36:37.420 --> 00:36:39.932
As if breaking bad news
isn't hard enough.

871
00:36:39.956 --> 00:36:43.737
Hey. Tiny Tim. Saint Nick.
hold up a minute.

872
00:36:43.760 --> 00:36:44.972
Sam. what are you doing here?

873
00:36:44.996 --> 00:36:47.594
Hey. uh. again. Sammy.
we're-we're sorry about yesterday.

874
00:36:47.617 --> 00:36:48.898
Don't be.

875
00:36:48.922 --> 00:36:50.911
You had the happiness
of so many kids at stake.

876
00:36:50.934 --> 00:36:54.347
Plus. gentlemen.
it is the season of forgiveness.

877
00:36:54.371 --> 00:36:56.382
- It sure is.
- DIANA: Excuse me?

878
00:36:56.407 --> 00:36:59.820
- Are you guys with NCIS?
- Two out of three.

879
00:36:59.844 --> 00:37:02.289
Special Agents McGee
and Torres. We're. uh...

880
00:37:02.313 --> 00:37:04.456
We're afraid that. uh. we have some news

881
00:37:04.481 --> 00:37:07.050
- on the stolen toys that's. uh...
- Wonderful?

882
00:37:07.730 --> 00:37:09.041
- What?
- DIANA: It's wonderful.

883
00:37:09.065 --> 00:37:11.610
It's a miracle. Come.

884
00:37:11.635 --> 00:37:13.813
Come see for yourself. Come.

885
00:37:13.990 --> 00:37:15.869
Show them. Show them. (LAUGHS)

886
00:37:15.893 --> 00:37:17.269
Ah.

887
00:37:17.293 --> 00:37:20.273
All of this was just delivered

888
00:37:20.297 --> 00:37:23.275
in what must've been five SUVs
by these men

889
00:37:23.300 --> 00:37:24.978
who were in quite a hurry.

890
00:37:25.001 --> 00:37:29.273
but they insisted that I make
sure that this card got to NCIS.

891
00:37:30.206 --> 00:37:33.621
"Sorry for the mix-up.
Apologies to Sammy.

892
00:37:33.644 --> 00:37:36.188
"The boys want no trouble from NCIS.

893
00:37:36.213 --> 00:37:37.556
Happy Holidays. T."

894
00:37:37.581 --> 00:37:39.326
TORRES: Even bad guys love Christmas.

895
00:37:39.349 --> 00:37:40.927
MCGEE: Wait a second. is "T"

896
00:37:40.952 --> 00:37:44.164
your very scary man?
And "the boys" are his boys?

897
00:37:44.188 --> 00:37:47.391
You know what? I'm gonna
plead the Fifth on that.

898
00:37:49.126 --> 00:37:52.204
- Well. then. you know what?
- We're gonna let you.

899
00:37:52.228 --> 00:37:54.474
Hey. guys. would it be too much to ask

900
00:37:54.498 --> 00:37:56.902
if you could possibly help
bring the gifts over?

901
00:37:56.925 --> 00:37:58.014
Oh. yeah. absolutely.

902
00:37:58.038 --> 00:37:59.045
- Sure. sure.
- Good.

903
00:37:59.070 --> 00:38:02.114
Oh. and actually. come to think of it.

904
00:38:02.139 --> 00:38:04.050
um. our Santa called in sick.

905
00:38:04.074 --> 00:38:07.744
but we still have the red suit. so...?

906
00:38:08.612 --> 00:38:09.822
(DIANA LAUGHS)

907
00:38:09.847 --> 00:38:12.960
- Ho ho ho!
- Who wants to see Santa?

908
00:38:12.983 --> 00:38:15.996
- Merry Christmas.
- Who wants to see Santa?

909
00:38:16.019 --> 00:38:17.898
- (CHILDREN CHEERING)
- SAMMY: Santa's here!

910
00:38:17.922 --> 00:38:19.791
Come here.

911
00:38:20.858 --> 00:38:23.059
♪ ♪

912
00:38:41.945 --> 00:38:44.391
Sammy. As promised.

913
00:38:44.414 --> 00:38:45.893
Here you go. boss.

914
00:38:45.916 --> 00:38:47.894
Yeah. Least we could do.

915
00:38:47.918 --> 00:38:49.353
Very true.

916
00:38:50.153 --> 00:38:51.271
Thanks. guys.

917
00:38:51.297 --> 00:38:54.434
My niece is gonna think
I am the greatest uncle...

918
00:39:00.182 --> 00:39:01.327
Merry Christmas. kid.

919
00:39:01.351 --> 00:39:03.097
Enjoy your Blaze the Dragon.

920
00:39:03.233 --> 00:39:04.376
- Hey.
- Aww.

921
00:39:04.400 --> 00:39:05.802
(TORRES CHUCKLES)

922
00:39:09.907 --> 00:39:11.342
(KNOCKING ON DOOR)

923
00:39:12.143 --> 00:39:13.420
Parker. Come on in.

924
00:39:13.443 --> 00:39:15.222
- I just need a minute.
- Of course.

925
00:39:15.503 --> 00:39:17.757
Sorry. I've. uh...

926
00:39:17.782 --> 00:39:19.927
been slammed a bit here with work.

927
00:39:19.951 --> 00:39:21.929
Any luck with the old state trooper?

928
00:39:21.952 --> 00:39:23.320
Luck?

929
00:39:25.061 --> 00:39:27.163
Guess you could call it that.

930
00:39:28.251 --> 00:39:29.862
(SIGHS)

931
00:39:30.226 --> 00:39:31.594
What is it?

932
00:39:33.297 --> 00:39:34.807
Is there anything I can do?

933
00:39:34.831 --> 00:39:37.277
Yeah. you can secure me a dive team

934
00:39:37.300 --> 00:39:41.271
to dredge a small pond
outside of Toledo. Ohio.

935
00:39:42.005 --> 00:39:47.210
Looking for a submerged
1969 Dodge Coronet.

936
00:39:47.797 --> 00:39:49.231
You got it.

937
00:39:53.250 --> 00:39:55.327
Oh. and I think the. uh.

938
00:39:55.351 --> 00:39:59.322
interrogation room camera
might be on the fritz.

939
00:40:14.137 --> 00:40:15.505
(SIGHS)

940
00:40:17.041 --> 00:40:19.286
Any words from your sister?

941
00:40:19.309 --> 00:40:21.487
Uh. lots of them.

942
00:40:21.512 --> 00:40:23.990
Spoke for three hours last night.

943
00:40:24.014 --> 00:40:25.657
She's still at sea till next week.

944
00:40:25.681 --> 00:40:29.161
but I might take some lost time
to go and see her.

945
00:40:29.186 --> 00:40:31.498
No "might" about that. You go.

946
00:40:31.521 --> 00:40:32.923
(PARKER SIGHS)

947
00:40:43.333 --> 00:40:46.036
♪ ♪

948
00:40:53.248 --> 00:40:54.885
You good?

949
00:40:56.914 --> 00:40:58.715
Yeah. I'm great.

950
00:41:01.403 --> 00:41:02.847
(SIGHS)

951
00:41:02.873 --> 00:41:05.385
Jess. you've been a real friend
through all of this.

952
00:41:05.409 --> 00:41:07.755
so... thank you.

953
00:41:07.925 --> 00:41:09.992
Nothing you wouldn't have done.

954
00:41:10.760 --> 00:41:12.163
Just like your mother.

955
00:41:13.364 --> 00:41:15.599
Well. she's finally home.

956
00:41:16.900 --> 00:41:18.878
Barring any more curveballs.

957
00:41:18.902 --> 00:41:21.181
Oh. bite your tongue.

958
00:41:21.204 --> 00:41:23.150
(PHONE RINGING)

959
00:41:23.173 --> 00:41:24.351
I did it. didn't I?

960
00:41:24.375 --> 00:41:26.010
- I spoke too soon.
- Yeah. Mm-hmm.

961
00:41:26.943 --> 00:41:28.688
Oh. it's just Director Vance.

962
00:41:28.918 --> 00:41:30.820
Excuse me.

963
00:41:31.882 --> 00:41:33.065
Director?

964
00:41:33.088 --> 00:41:34.894
<i>VANCE: Hope I'm not
interrupting the service.</i>

965
00:41:34.918 --> 00:41:37.264
Uh. no. but it's just about to start.

966
00:41:37.288 --> 00:41:38.731
<i>All right. then. I'll be quick.</i>

967
00:41:38.755 --> 00:41:40.934
<i>You got your wish. Agent Knight.</i>

968
00:41:40.958 --> 00:41:43.070
<i>NCIS: ELITE just got orders.</i>

969
00:41:43.094 --> 00:41:45.572
<i>There's a wanted fugitive
you'll be tracking.</i>

970
00:41:45.596 --> 00:41:47.539
Finally.

971
00:41:47.563 --> 00:41:49.742
Yes. sir. What's the target?

972
00:41:49.766 --> 00:41:51.378
<i>Well. that's why I'm calling.</i>

973
00:41:51.402 --> 00:41:53.880
<i>since this one hits
a little close to home.</i>

974
00:41:53.905 --> 00:41:55.072
(PHONE CHIMES)

975
00:42:07.985 --> 00:42:09.963
That's the target?

976
00:42:09.987 --> 00:42:12.298
<i>Details to follow from your CO.</i>

977
00:42:12.322 --> 00:42:15.659
<i>Until then.
this stays between us.</i>

978
00:42:16.559 --> 00:42:18.161
Understood. sir.

979
00:42:20.297 --> 00:42:21.831
(EXHALES)
